WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.570
The use effect hook.

1
00:00:02.839 --> 00:00:03.900
What is use effect?

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.840
The use effect hook and react

3
00:00:06.079 --> 00:00:08.909
allows you to perform side effects in functional components.

4
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:12.300
A side effect can be anything that interacts with the outside world

5
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.680
or modifies something outside the component's render process.

6
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.489
Examples include

7
00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.290
fetching data from an API,

8
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.819
subscribing to events,

9
00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:22.510
manipulating the DOM,

10
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.670
or setting up and cleaning up timers.

11
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.020
Why do we use use effect?

12
00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:28.649
Before hooks,

13
00:00:29.049 --> 00:00:33.299
side effects and react were handled using life cycle methods and class components,

14
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.310
such as component did mount,

15
00:00:35.689 --> 00:00:37.060
Component did update,

16
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:39.049
and component will unmount.

17
00:00:39.799 --> 00:00:40.590
With hooks,

18
00:00:40.919 --> 00:00:43.659
use effect combines all these life cycle methods

19
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.099
into a single function.

20
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:47.889
Basic syntax.

21
00:00:48.689 --> 00:00:51.810
The use effect hook takes a function as its first argument,

22
00:00:52.169 --> 00:00:52.810
type,

23
00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:54.169
use effect,

24
00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:55.669
open parentheses,

25
00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:56.650
function,

26
00:00:57.049 --> 00:00:58.130
close parentheses.

27
00:00:58.799 --> 00:01:00.580
It can also take a second argument,

28
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.319
a dependency array.

29
00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.449
The dependency array

30
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.519
controls when the effects should run.

31
00:01:07.069 --> 00:01:08.489
No dependency array.

32
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.949
The effect runs on every render.

33
00:01:11.769 --> 00:01:13.529
Empty dependency array.

34
00:01:13.889 --> 00:01:16.529
The effect runs only once after the initial render.

35
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.550
With dependencies,

36
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.059
the effect runs only when the specified dependencies change.

37
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.949
Let's look at an example.

38
00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:25.819
Runs on every render,

39
00:01:26.089 --> 00:01:27.370
no dependency array.

40
00:01:29.319 --> 00:01:30.400
Runs only once,

41
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.040
empty dependency array.

42
00:01:37.139 --> 00:01:38.839
Runs only when the count changes.

43
00:01:39.519 --> 00:01:41.199
Dependency array with count.

44
00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:43.470
Cleaning up use effect.

45
00:01:44.599 --> 00:01:45.190
Sometimes,

46
00:01:45.199 --> 00:01:48.110
side effects require cleanup to avoid memory leaks.

47
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.000
For example,

48
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.879
removing event listeners or clearing timers.

49
00:01:52.629 --> 00:01:53.389
To handle this,

50
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.120
you can return a cleanup function from use effect.

51
00:01:56.809 --> 00:01:57.720
Here's an example.

52
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:00.129
A timer is set using set interval.

53
00:02:00.769 --> 00:02:01.559
The cleanup function,

54
00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:02.529
which is returned,

55
00:02:02.809 --> 00:02:04.980
calls clear interval to stop the timer.

56
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.169
It also logs a message to the console to indicate the cleanup.

57
00:02:11.649 --> 00:02:13.210
That's all for this lesson.

58
00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.809
See you in the next lesson and have a good day.